Design:

```ts
export function dedupe<K extends string, V>(fn: (key: K) => Promise<V>): (key: K) => Promise<V> {
  const inflight = new Map<string, Promise<V>>();
  return (key) => {
    const existing = inflight.get(key);
    if (existing) return existing;
    const p = fn(key).finally(() => {
      if (inflight.get(key) === p) inflight.delete(key);
    });
    inflight.set(key, p);
    return p;
  };
}
```

Note: .finally creates a new promise; rejection propagates but unhandled rejection on the inner promise? Actually the returned promise is `p` (the finally-derived one), and we store that in map. The original promise's rejection is handled by the finally chain — fine. But wait: if two callers await p, both get rejection. Good. Rejection not cached since map cleared on settle. Good.

One subtlety: `.finally` callback runs when settled; deleting only if map entry is still p (avoid deleting a newer entry if... actually can't happen synchronously, but safe anyway).

